message.content:
OK so `return_trace` just returns the value. Now let me understand the vulnerability better.

Let me look at the `hb_array_t::copy` return type. It returns `hb_array_t`. But in `UnsizedArrayOf::copy`:

```cpp
UnsizedArrayOf* copy (hb_serialize_context_t *c, unsigned count) const
{
    TRACE_SERIALIZE (this);
    auto *out = c->start_embed (this);
    if (unlikely (!as_array (count).copy (c))) return_trace (nullptr);
    return_trace (out);
}
```

The `if (unlikely (!as_array(count).copy(c)))` - this calls `hb_array_t::copy` which returns an `hb_array_t`. The `!` operator on `hb_array_t` - what does it do?

